[{:db/id "q-90694/selection/782f9fdb0c37b7131c5e22cb0b749c7e3657532bc2175a370aa83d8ddd228f67" :answer/id #uuid "400205ac-0e74-5985-bb4a-66b94fb18dd5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$1\\,764$ people" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-90694/selection/bb67e0be4bf3be64b579ed430a9c9d1981c7d023f307e6dbc9c0b163597b0dc1" :answer/id #uuid "84975ce5-cf1a-5175-8980-b8d402dd8c78" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$4\\,671$ people" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-90694/selection/934c0b77c691dbd1fb075c32e1eba0db887408f3099e61d8a358a1f56c3a1e6b" :answer/id #uuid "7d795093-08f1-5af2-977f-da15b141598d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$7\\,461$ people" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-90694/selection/07f3d48d686727a9030bd693248a30593ec8f6c81017515da14635d553e060ce" :answer/id #uuid "414b0cc8-fd63-5f5f-b31f-a1de4efa6174" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$6\\,471$ people" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-90694/selection/63279789799346ab29fc296403ce2ccff8af12816a8ecd6244099306caf07ade" :answer/id #uuid "2b6e9c1f-3046-5b81-a670-b6b672045f9c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$1\\,467$ people" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-90694/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "08d1c4ae-a1b0-52f6-82a6-021f81d038d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-90694/selection/bb67e0be4bf3be64b579ed430a9c9d1981c7d023f307e6dbc9c0b163597b0dc1" :answer-field/choices ["q-90694/selection/782f9fdb0c37b7131c5e22cb0b749c7e3657532bc2175a370aa83d8ddd228f67" "q-90694/selection/bb67e0be4bf3be64b579ed430a9c9d1981c7d023f307e6dbc9c0b163597b0dc1" "q-90694/selection/934c0b77c691dbd1fb075c32e1eba0db887408f3099e61d8a358a1f56c3a1e6b" "q-90694/selection/07f3d48d686727a9030bd693248a30593ec8f6c81017515da14635d553e060ce" "q-90694/selection/63279789799346ab29fc296403ce2ccff8af12816a8ecd6244099306caf07ade"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-90694" :question/id #uuid "a4a96f45-52c2-52f7-a934-05bbe7001e15" :question/math-academy-id "q-90694" :question/problem "Since $2005$, the population of a certain town grows at a rate $r(t)$ given by the function \n \n\n$$\nr(t)=200{e}^{0.2t}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in people per year, and $t>0$ is the time in years since $2005.$\n\nTo the nearest integer, by how many people did the population of the town increase between the years $2010$ and $2015?$" :question/worked-solution "We're given the **rate of change** in population $r(t).$ To find the change in  population between  $2010$ and $2015$, we integrate $r(t)$ between the$t=5$ and $t=10.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{5}^{10}200{e}^{0.2t}\\,\\text{d}t & =200\\cdot {{e}^{0.2t}|}_{5}^{10} \\\\  & =1\\,000\\,{{e}^{0.2t}|}_{5}^{10} \\\\  & =1\\,000({e}^{2}-{e}^{1}) \\\\  & ≈4\\,671\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.  \n\nTherefore, between  $2010$ and $2015$ the population of the town increased by $4\\,671$ people." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-90694/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b19641aa-8e39-5348-8565-9f4ce030d18f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-90694"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-62799"] :question/problem "A water tank is filling with water. The rate $r(t)$ at which water enters the tank is given by the function \n\n$$\nr(t)=20{e}^{-2t},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in liters per minute, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes since the tank started filling up. \n\nTo one decimal place, how much water enters the tank between $t=1$ minute and $t=3$ minutes?" :question/worked-solution "We're given the **rate of change** of volume $r(t).$ To find the **net change** in volume between the times $t=1$ and $t=3,$ we integrate $r(t)$ between these two limits. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}20{e}^{-2t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={-\\frac{20}{2}{e}^{-2t}|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & ={-10{e}^{-2t}|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =-10({e}^{-6}-{e}^{-2}) \\\\  & ≈1.3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place. \n\nTherefore, between $t=1$ minute and $t=3$ minutes, $1.3$ liters of water entered the tank." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-349771/field-1/f1c75845439f7547b2a89eaac59f436d7ce65dcfcf9ca28c1b66d939a351c89c" :answer/id #uuid "b43db25e-5a81-5937-89e3-1810b7e9a2c1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "312" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-349771/field-1/bd6a8fadee21d3bae00ca3beb5bb88107d613102577da4647e80fb7b41fbe935" :answer/id #uuid "15210147-c88f-56d1-a200-c01adc83666e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "216" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-349771/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "72b67fe2-2cef-5392-b8f4-229634d35295" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-349771/field-1/f1c75845439f7547b2a89eaac59f436d7ce65dcfcf9ca28c1b66d939a351c89c" :answer-field/choices ["q-349771/field-1/f1c75845439f7547b2a89eaac59f436d7ce65dcfcf9ca28c1b66d939a351c89c" "q-349771/field-1/bd6a8fadee21d3bae00ca3beb5bb88107d613102577da4647e80fb7b41fbe935"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-349771" :question/id #uuid "0a01a9ef-4113-5c96-9cb0-3af4ac0237c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-349771" :question/problem "A website is tracking the rate at which new users sign up. The rate $r(t)$ at which users sign up is given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=12\\cdot {2}^{t/3},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in users per day, and $t>0$ is the time in days.\n\nBetween $t=3$ and $t=9$ days, to nearest integer,\n{{field-1}} users signed up." :question/worked-solution "We are given the signup rate $r(t)$ in users per day. To find the number of users that signed up between the times $t=3$ and $t=9$ days, we integrate $r(t)$ between these two limits. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{9}12\\cdot {2}^{t/3}\\,\\text{d}t & ={12\\cdot \\frac{3\\cdot {2}^{t/3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}|}_{3}^{9} \\\\  & ={36\\cdot \\frac{{2}^{t/3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}|}_{3}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}({2}^{3}-{2}^{1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{216}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & ≈312\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer. \n\nTherefore, between $t=3$ and $t=9$ days, $312$ users signed up." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-349771/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b19641aa-8e39-5348-8565-9f4ce030d18f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-349771"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-349765/field-1/e916c8a7feeda411fe779cd5fe7c4b17e218edc95634e3bb39e4becf913f78b0" :answer/id #uuid "806e8fa2-8a1b-55f9-8e25-eb19f18feea4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "14.0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-349765/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "943d9af1-c345-5fad-9fb1-ae42487951cb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-349765/field-1/e916c8a7feeda411fe779cd5fe7c4b17e218edc95634e3bb39e4becf913f78b0" :answer-field/choices ["q-349765/field-1/e916c8a7feeda411fe779cd5fe7c4b17e218edc95634e3bb39e4becf913f78b0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-349765" :question/id #uuid "a131754b-a4fe-55c0-993a-b216dfd3a19f" :question/math-academy-id "q-349765" :question/problem "A concert venue is tracking the rate at which guests enter the building. The rate $r(t)$ at which guests arrive is given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=3\\sqrt{t+1},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in guests per minute, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes.\n\nBetween $t=0$ and $t=3$ minutes, to one decimal place,\n{{field-1}} guests arrived." :question/worked-solution "We're given the **rate of change** in the number of guests, $r(t).$ To find the number of guests that arrived between the times $t=0$ and $t=3$ minutes, we integrate $r(t)$ between these two limits. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}3\\sqrt{t+1}\\,\\text{d}t & =3\\,\\frac{2(t+1)^{3/2}}{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =2(t+1)^{3/2}\\,{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =2(3+1)^{3/2}-2(0+1)^{3/2} \\\\  & =2(4)^{3/2}-2(1)^{3/2} \\\\  & =16-2 \\\\  & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, between $t=0$ and $t=3$ minutes, $14$ guests arrived." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-349765/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b19641aa-8e39-5348-8565-9f4ce030d18f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-349765"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-349752/field-1/12b48b00f9c1ff14570ca66bc629787a88856efba1cda24cfb28da58c148801a" :answer/id #uuid "da9fff78-dc71-5410-b972-a7f966712ae2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "22" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-349752/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "5c6fb7b5-de3b-53c0-bf16-23243a0bf349" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-349752/field-1/12b48b00f9c1ff14570ca66bc629787a88856efba1cda24cfb28da58c148801a" :answer-field/choices ["q-349752/field-1/12b48b00f9c1ff14570ca66bc629787a88856efba1cda24cfb28da58c148801a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-349752" :question/id #uuid "ba53887f-6b38-5495-ab47-7768f2a88cd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-349752" :question/problem "A laboratory is tracking the rate at which a chemical is being produced in a reaction. The rate $r(t)$ at which the chemical is produced is given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=2{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+4,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in milligrams per hour, and $0<t<5$ is the time in hours since $1\\,\\text{pm}.$\n\nBetween $t=1$ and $t=3$ hours,\n{{field-1}} milligrams were produced." :question/worked-solution "We're given the **rate of change** of chemical production $r(t).$ To find the **net amount** of chemical produced by the reaction between the times $t=1$ and $t=3,$ we integrate $r(t)$ between these two limits. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(2{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}t & ={(\\frac{2}{4}{t}^{4}-\\frac{3}{3}{t}^{3}+4t)|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{4}-{t}^{3}+4t)|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2}(3)^{4}-(3)^{3}+4(3))-(\\frac{1}{2}(1)^{4}-(1)^{3}+4(1)) \\\\  & =(\\frac{81}{2}-27+12)-(\\frac{1}{2}-1+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{51}{2}-\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, between $t=1$ and $t=3$ hours, $22$ milligrams were produced." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-349752/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b19641aa-8e39-5348-8565-9f4ce030d18f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-349752"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-62825/selection/5edda82aefdaf5d9c71bad9a81d16031535c5af7dec972bb25c1ead521683f62" :answer/id #uuid "d59deb8e-814c-501a-a210-68f42f7751ea" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$350$ liters" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-62825/selection/97dbe711de0bdfc1045dba532b60618d32575bb2910c46b29658d26437e5e62e" :answer/id #uuid "fa29857f-bd00-5600-80b8-f5b60ceaeee8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$3\\,160$ liters" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-62825/selection/367236df5b9767c8e0ec83202a8fb0308258ecd0d8df1db34ca6f737950ed11c" :answer/id #uuid "f43cd29c-fa96-5b19-9eab-68c6b962799c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$3\\,020$ liters" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-62825/selection/4800159f9bac3cc2e396ab82a890b0f9f983c6b7cec91c835d6ee05fc828caab" :answer/id #uuid "c9353486-e1ee-598a-bb98-f05c8cad5b32" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$3\\,140$ liters" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-62825/selection/6f7e663ea542c3ded0a1cec01540d019ae95c6e12369ba95c29437b84b9aaee0" :answer/id #uuid "fcd9947c-2fac-56af-a9a4-4bc16fab8efd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$250$ liters" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-62825/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "da9ee08b-d711-541b-924d-6a8909b280fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-62825/selection/97dbe711de0bdfc1045dba532b60618d32575bb2910c46b29658d26437e5e62e" :answer-field/choices ["q-62825/selection/5edda82aefdaf5d9c71bad9a81d16031535c5af7dec972bb25c1ead521683f62" "q-62825/selection/97dbe711de0bdfc1045dba532b60618d32575bb2910c46b29658d26437e5e62e" "q-62825/selection/367236df5b9767c8e0ec83202a8fb0308258ecd0d8df1db34ca6f737950ed11c" "q-62825/selection/4800159f9bac3cc2e396ab82a890b0f9f983c6b7cec91c835d6ee05fc828caab" "q-62825/selection/6f7e663ea542c3ded0a1cec01540d019ae95c6e12369ba95c29437b84b9aaee0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-62825" :question/id #uuid "d586afc6-dd55-5a3f-a72a-36aae91b1948" :question/math-academy-id "q-62825" :question/problem "A tank has an inlet and an outlet. When the inlet is turned on, the rate at which water flows into the tank governed by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=9{t}^{2}+18,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in liters per minute and $t>0$ is the time in minutes.\n\nWhen the outlet is switched on, water leaves the tank through it at a rate of $14$ liters per minute.\n\nThe inlet is switched on, and the water level increases to $120$ liters, at which point the outlet is also switched on. How much water is in the tank $10$ minutes after switching on the outlet, given that the outlet is switched on at $t=0?$" :question/worked-solution "Water enters the tank at a rate of $9{t}^{2}+18$ liters per minute and leaves at a rate of $14$ liters per minute. Therefore, the **rate of change of volume** (call it $R(t)$) is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =r(t)-14 \\\\  & =9{t}^{2}+18-14 \\\\  & =9{t}^{2}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the **total volume** of water in the tank after $t$ minutes (call it $T(t)$), we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =T(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}R(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =120+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(9{x}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that $T$ is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration. \n\nTo find out the volume of water after $10$ minutes, we substitute $t=10$ into the above and evaluate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(10) & =120+{\\int }_{0}^{10}(9{x}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =120+{[3{x}^{3}+4x]|}_{0}^{10} \\\\  & =120+[(3(10)^{3}+4(10))-(0)] \\\\  & =120+3040 \\\\  & =3\\,160\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-62825/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f692eea3-1a29-5067-86dd-16a56be40703"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-62825"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-62808/selection/e481a0358bd89887e205d5181ca7672a5a634f90d77d1f1a87604a5167d02581" :answer/id #uuid "8ab46693-d9e9-5bbc-ab14-8c8ccf17fcfa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{t}(50+\\frac{500}{1+2x})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-62808/selection/5c31bb3991edab80bfdf728ca705c750f84f40fea6fcb4e002ed2c858de78bee" :answer/id #uuid "5abaf428-9128-5508-9dd9-aa296fc9bc1f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{50}(50+\\frac{500}{1+2x})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-62808/selection/e8d5bc60f4c7f0d87e4eb31f19074b35346479224d22ecb0a6735059dc699709" :answer/id #uuid "d723c0b9-b110-5cdb-b094-294e63aef077" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "50+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\frac{500}{1+2x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-62808/selection/eac1bb1a49d222d3a45eaeb80bf40fc6d40fe77ec5696057e6dbc5c90df98606" :answer/id #uuid "f204ac6e-eeec-5e73-8706-12bcea70a8ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{50}\\frac{500}{1+2x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-62808/selection/64fec68c595356591d849e030330c42dd3df96ce58a1e9da2482cb6d5f91b0a6" :answer/id #uuid "a82a9ab8-f33c-5a5a-9c74-3e91f5887df5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\frac{500}{1+2x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-62808/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f4618cc4-ad1a-58f8-883f-8095cb379739" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-62808/selection/e8d5bc60f4c7f0d87e4eb31f19074b35346479224d22ecb0a6735059dc699709" :answer-field/choices ["q-62808/selection/e481a0358bd89887e205d5181ca7672a5a634f90d77d1f1a87604a5167d02581" "q-62808/selection/5c31bb3991edab80bfdf728ca705c750f84f40fea6fcb4e002ed2c858de78bee" "q-62808/selection/e8d5bc60f4c7f0d87e4eb31f19074b35346479224d22ecb0a6735059dc699709" "q-62808/selection/eac1bb1a49d222d3a45eaeb80bf40fc6d40fe77ec5696057e6dbc5c90df98606" "q-62808/selection/64fec68c595356591d849e030330c42dd3df96ce58a1e9da2482cb6d5f91b0a6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-62808" :question/id #uuid "e5e01b2b-59f7-5c9b-a441-1c8b40304b9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-62808" :question/problem "Air is being pumped into a tire. The rate $r(t)$ at which air enters the tire is given by a function\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\frac{500}{1+2t},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in ${\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{min},$ and $t>0$ is the time in minutes. Initially, there was $50\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}$ of air in the tire.\n\nWhich integral gives the volume of air in the tire $t$ minutes since the pumping started?" :question/worked-solution "We're given the **rate of change of volume** $r(t).$ To find the **total volume** after $t$ minutes (call it $V(t)$), we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(t) & =V(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\frac{500}{1+2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =50+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\frac{500}{1+2x}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that our final function is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-62808/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f692eea3-1a29-5067-86dd-16a56be40703"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-62808"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-62803/selection/f46d524e55407e255be99f50d55e64529f5786656be44a03741db6c22486129b" :answer/id #uuid "0179ded7-7fbb-57c8-88ff-0a09a373a653" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$291\\,000$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-62803/selection/efa35cb892d9dfe0511e7d4c195fe270fe4089f9df1e1ceab6a0d8951c8da0ca" :answer/id #uuid "199f4fdd-1033-5100-ac09-fdfa74ed8a40" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$181\\,000$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-62803/selection/99b32c2e470de7512e046a62146dbe5f17c78dbd6e42356cb89fe52709469eff" :answer/id #uuid "d3a87bff-3280-5f01-a29e-80b75cff73ae" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$176\\,000$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-62803/selection/584b3bdb6534f1af030f4ed6c3469de4fc872e342e5d10769c49d9e6279f9010" :answer/id #uuid "f2a1c8b7-76c4-5c9d-99cb-a2ea596f9766" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$402\\,000$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-62803/selection/a892b91dbf549867100cec3c913778e8e8a68f4654ac1b04da5d60f6dc994855" :answer/id #uuid "42557b93-ee36-5620-a129-49c0669d5e75" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$185\\,000$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-62803/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "46101e9a-4ad7-572e-b200-2c703b884679" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-62803/selection/efa35cb892d9dfe0511e7d4c195fe270fe4089f9df1e1ceab6a0d8951c8da0ca" :answer-field/choices ["q-62803/selection/f46d524e55407e255be99f50d55e64529f5786656be44a03741db6c22486129b" "q-62803/selection/efa35cb892d9dfe0511e7d4c195fe270fe4089f9df1e1ceab6a0d8951c8da0ca" "q-62803/selection/99b32c2e470de7512e046a62146dbe5f17c78dbd6e42356cb89fe52709469eff" "q-62803/selection/584b3bdb6534f1af030f4ed6c3469de4fc872e342e5d10769c49d9e6279f9010" "q-62803/selection/a892b91dbf549867100cec3c913778e8e8a68f4654ac1b04da5d60f6dc994855"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-62803" :question/id #uuid "6d5889d4-4ad2-5352-a1b9-05ab5c8271c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-62803" :question/problem "In a particular business bank account, the rate at which money is being deposited into the account is given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=600(2t+1)^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in dollars per month, and $t>0$ is the number of months since the account was opened. When the account was first opened, the business owners placed an initial deposit of $$1\\,000$ into the account.\n\nHow much money will be in the account after $3$ months?" :question/worked-solution "We're given a **rate of change** in the amount of money $r(t).$ \n\nTo find the **total amount** of money in the account after $t$ months (call it $T(t)$), we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =T(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}r(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1\\,000+{\\int }_{0}^{t}600(2x+1)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo be sure that $T$ is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration. \n\nWe now let $t=3,$ and evaluate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(3) & =1\\,000+{\\int }_{0}^{3}600(2x+1)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1\\,000+600{\\int }_{0}^{3}(2x+1)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1\\,000+\\frac{600}{2\\cdot 4}{(2t+1)^{4}|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =1\\,000+75{(2t+1)^{4}|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =1\\,000+75[(2(3)+1)^{4}-(2(0)+1)^{4}] \\\\  & =1\\,000+75[{7}^{4}-{1}^{4}] \\\\  & =1\\,000+75[2\\,401-1] \\\\  & =1\\,000+75[2\\,400] \\\\  & =181\\,000\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there will be $$181\\,000$ in the account after $3$ months." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-62803/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f692eea3-1a29-5067-86dd-16a56be40703"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-62803"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-349982/field-1/3afc3280cb8820370064548b7679cc3cf959ee570b3df1cb4ef9b7e3a7cdd717" :answer/id #uuid "3683d4f0-3594-5b70-b1f4-51c97bc23549" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8(t+4)^{3/2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-349982/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "be2e369f-7e27-552c-b6a7-966e138c296c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-349982/field-1/3afc3280cb8820370064548b7679cc3cf959ee570b3df1cb4ef9b7e3a7cdd717" :answer-field/choices ["q-349982/field-1/3afc3280cb8820370064548b7679cc3cf959ee570b3df1cb4ef9b7e3a7cdd717"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-349982" :question/id #uuid "013c80a2-1889-52a0-8325-437967b02894" :question/math-academy-id "q-349982" :question/problem "Engineers are testing a water pump filling a reservoir.\nThey observe that water enters the reservoir at a rate $r(t)$ given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=12\\sqrt{t+4},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in gallons per minute, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes since the pump was turned on.\nAt the same time, water leaks from the reservoir at a constant rate of $6$ gallons per minute.\n\nIf the reservoir was initially empty, find the expression that gives $V(t),$ the volume of water in the reservoir $t$ minutes after the pump was turned on.\n\n$V(t)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-\\,6t-64$" :question/worked-solution "Water is entering the reservoir at a rate of $r(t)$ gallons per minute and is leaking at a rate of $6$ gallons per minute. Therefore, the **rate of change** $R(t)$ of the volume of water is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =r(t)-6 \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{t+4}-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the total volume $V(t)$ after $t$ minutes, we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(t) & =V(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}R(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}(12\\sqrt{x+4}-6)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that our final function is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration. Also, since the reservoir is initially empty, we set $V(0)=0.$\n\nWe can simplify the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}12\\sqrt{x+4}\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{t}6\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={12\\cdot \\frac{2(x+4)^{3/2}}{3}|}_{0}^{t}-6x{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =8(t+4)^{3/2}-8(4)^{3/2}-6t \\\\  & =8(t+4)^{3/2}-6t-64\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-349982/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f692eea3-1a29-5067-86dd-16a56be40703"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-349982"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-349983/field-1/6d3c8442cc3b22e366390d78310735135373e8ecad8ba4c6ba0de760b06b227b" :answer/id #uuid "cb0759ef-2195-5981-bac6-3f816302add9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "260-3t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-349983/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "2f4af640-6fc4-561c-9b7f-ce62899e2c1b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-349983/field-1/6d3c8442cc3b22e366390d78310735135373e8ecad8ba4c6ba0de760b06b227b" :answer-field/choices ["q-349983/field-1/6d3c8442cc3b22e366390d78310735135373e8ecad8ba4c6ba0de760b06b227b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-349983" :question/id #uuid "8b26926d-a4cd-5562-8160-9ecf1e52b47d" :question/math-academy-id "q-349983" :question/problem "A zoo is tracking the number of penguins in a habitat.\nThey observe that new penguins arrive at a rate $r(t)$ given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=30{e}^{-t/2},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in penguins per month, and $t>0$ is the time in months since the tracking began.\nAt the same time, $3$ penguins are transferred out each month.\n\nIf there were initially $200$ penguins in the habitat, find the expression that gives $P(t),$ the number of penguins in the habitat $t$ months after the tracking began.\n\n$P(t)=-60{e}^{-t/2}\\,+$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The number of penguins is increasing at a rate of $r(t)$ penguins per month and is decreasing at a rate of $3$ penguins per month. Therefore, the **rate of change** $R(t)$ of the number of penguins is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =r(t)-3 \\\\  & =30{e}^{-t/2}-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the total number $P(t)$ of penguins after $t$ months, we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(t) & =P(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}R(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =200+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(30{e}^{-x/2}-3)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that our final function is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration.\n\nWe can simplify the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(t) & =200+{\\int }_{0}^{t}30{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{t}3\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =200+(-60{e}^{-x/2}){|}_{0}^{t}-3x{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =200+(60-60{e}^{-t/2})-3t \\\\  & =-60{e}^{-t/2}+260-3t\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-349983/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f692eea3-1a29-5067-86dd-16a56be40703"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-349983"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-90756/selection/c7347bc2d2b7d27c6b2358d0df7db07db0ec3face8e089af36857b365ed27292" :answer/id #uuid "08b6b69c-17a2-55fb-9c59-8d94ac8cd910" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The minimum is $230$ fish after $5$ weeks" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-90756/selection/18ee234fe183ebd98bdaccbcf9a9632a66645d9a1167dbf6809def1b19ae141f" :answer/id #uuid "033ff1f2-bc5c-526d-bf0c-e98c809d74d2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The minimum is $218$ fish after $2$ weeks" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-90756/selection/acd7d0477900a916091f17bd88b2f7ac8680767cdce2e798b04b0a0d712a62cb" :answer/id #uuid "d11448c1-7a0b-5332-8af2-5f845bd25322" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The minimum is $230$ fish after $1$ week" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-90756/selection/a3b121a25cbe262eaffdef369091766cf7a9b762344e083af4cfdfac8224dfa8" :answer/id #uuid "d298d34e-e56c-5372-8be8-374d47c321bf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The minimum is $215$ fish after $4$ weeks" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-90756/selection/baeae9e95e0e178c08b4f304341efad3725377667f50e2ac30999e4a4e4e127b" :answer/id #uuid "7e8957a5-f12f-5e4f-bdd3-4b6816480cf5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The minimum is $214$ fish after $3$ weeks" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-90756/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "041d2d0e-c5b1-5127-beca-eddf31b90a74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-90756/selection/baeae9e95e0e178c08b4f304341efad3725377667f50e2ac30999e4a4e4e127b" :answer-field/choices ["q-90756/selection/c7347bc2d2b7d27c6b2358d0df7db07db0ec3face8e089af36857b365ed27292" "q-90756/selection/18ee234fe183ebd98bdaccbcf9a9632a66645d9a1167dbf6809def1b19ae141f" "q-90756/selection/acd7d0477900a916091f17bd88b2f7ac8680767cdce2e798b04b0a0d712a62cb" "q-90756/selection/a3b121a25cbe262eaffdef369091766cf7a9b762344e083af4cfdfac8224dfa8" "q-90756/selection/baeae9e95e0e178c08b4f304341efad3725377667f50e2ac30999e4a4e4e127b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-90756" :question/id #uuid "3e88d227-68c0-5aae-9197-414a3be89369" :question/math-academy-id "q-90756" :question/problem "A man stocked a pond with $250$ fish. After $t$ weeks, the number of fish in the pond is given by the function \n\n$$\nF(t)=250+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(8x-24)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time (in weeks) since the man put the fish in the pond.\n\nAt what time is the number of fish in the pond at a minimum, and how many fish are there at that time?" :question/worked-solution "To find when the number of fish in the pond is at minimum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(250+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(8x-24)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(250)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({\\int }_{0}^{t}(8x-24)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(8x-24)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8t-24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=0$:\n\n$$\n8t-24=0\\,⟹\\,t=3\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=3.$ To find out how many fish are when $t=3,$ we figure out $F(3)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(3) & =250+{\\int }_{0}^{3}(8x-24)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =250{[4{x}^{2}-24x]|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =250+[(4(3)^{2}-24(3))-(0)] \\\\  & =250-36 \\\\  & =214\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $214<250$, we conclude that the minimum number of fish in the pond is $214$, and this will happen after $3$ weeks." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-90756/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3798ec35-19ef-559f-b8f8-cb0a0a03157b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-90756"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-349998/field-1/897ce4c7911f99b265ba23f864c4eaa9aec53942cbd2bbe07376f6b49ce12837" :answer/id #uuid "476d9191-f297-515c-91f8-2d46c90d9f09" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "95" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-349998/field-1/8642573b27c8383f67a645c39127977f20fbab89a940b170bac777f18f881b9b" :answer/id #uuid "77316c20-7e57-5ba1-8460-4790f097909a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "50" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-349998/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "c6828622-ccf7-5950-8053-3c586f9d3dba" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-349998/field-1/897ce4c7911f99b265ba23f864c4eaa9aec53942cbd2bbe07376f6b49ce12837" :answer-field/choices ["q-349998/field-1/897ce4c7911f99b265ba23f864c4eaa9aec53942cbd2bbe07376f6b49ce12837" "q-349998/field-1/8642573b27c8383f67a645c39127977f20fbab89a940b170bac777f18f881b9b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-349998/field-2/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "f55c4838-3d0f-5365-9107-79b9132bea42" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-349998/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "5a2004e8-77c6-580f-97d5-1bffa6e85a9d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-349998/field-2/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-349998/field-2/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-349998" :question/id #uuid "04d94105-9d71-5769-92eb-b32c2d959987" :question/math-academy-id "q-349998" :question/problem "On a certain afternoon, the total number of people $N(t)$ visiting the city's zoo is given by the function\n\n$$\nN(t)=50+5{\\int }_{0}^{t}(6-2x)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time (in hours) since noon.  \n\nAt what time is the number of people in the zoo at a maximum, and how many people are at the zoo at that time?\n\nThe maximum number of people is\n{{field-1}}\nafter\n{{field-2}}\nhours." :question/worked-solution "To find when the number of people at the zoo is at maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(50+5{\\int }_{0}^{t}(6-2x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(50)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5{\\int }_{0}^{t}(6-2x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =5\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(6-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5(6-2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=0$:\n\n$$\n5(6-2t)=0\\,⟹\\,t=3\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=3.$ To find out how many people are in the zoo when $t=3,$ we figure out $N(3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(3) & =50+5{\\int }_{0}^{3}(6-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =50+5{[6x-{x}^{2}]|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =50+5[(6\\cdot 3-{3}^{2})-(0)] \\\\  & =50+5\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =95\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $50<95$, we conclude that the maximum number of people at the zoo is $95$, and this will happen after $3$ hours." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-349998/field-1" "q-349998/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3798ec35-19ef-559f-b8f8-cb0a0a03157b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-349998"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-62828"] :question/problem "The total number of people $T(t)$ at a festival after $t$ hours is given by the function\n\n$$\nT(t)=10+{\\int }_{0}^{t}2x-4\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $T$ is measured in thousands of people, and $t>0$ is the time (in hours) since the festival opened its gates.\n\nAt what time is the number of people at the festival a minimum, and how many people are at the festival at this time?" :question/worked-solution "To find when the number of people is at a minimum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2+{\\int }_{0}^{t}2x-4\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({\\int }_{0}^{t}2x-4\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}2x-4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2t-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=0$:\n\n$$\n2t-4=0\\,⟹\\,t=2\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=2.$ To find out how many people are in the festival when $t=2,$ we figure out $T(2)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(2) & =10+{\\int }_{0}^{2}2x-4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10+{[{x}^{2}-4x]|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =10+[({2}^{2}-4(2))-(0)] \\\\  & =10-4 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $6\\,000<10\\,000$, we conclude that the minimum number of people is $6\\,000$, and this will happen after $2$ hours." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-349996/field-1/bb8a995520c357f67c022672647f2e790afb6cc163eefcc2ed9498c35e48e7b6" :answer/id #uuid "6d5919cb-6213-59cd-ac32-000a5f4b200d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "188" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-349996/field-1/772faee3af740960f7d296ca245df4da797a9e413368076cbaa828915d92739d" :answer/id #uuid "ba55c7e1-1193-5d21-bdbb-9efa3dcd4c2a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "128" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-349996/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "6abb0c3f-7763-55a7-8885-b51a45a0ee80" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-349996/field-1/bb8a995520c357f67c022672647f2e790afb6cc163eefcc2ed9498c35e48e7b6" :answer-field/choices ["q-349996/field-1/bb8a995520c357f67c022672647f2e790afb6cc163eefcc2ed9498c35e48e7b6" "q-349996/field-1/772faee3af740960f7d296ca245df4da797a9e413368076cbaa828915d92739d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-349996/field-2/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "1c58c9e5-6567-5e52-9805-a72c25e59b59" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-349996/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "4db061f9-7eb7-5fdd-b245-e885ae3fc59a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-349996/field-2/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-349996/field-2/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-349996" :question/id #uuid "176edced-7cbc-51f3-b6f0-1dfe994048e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-349996" :question/problem "On a certain afternoon, the total number of people $N(t)$ visiting the city's zoo is given by the function\n\n$$\nN(t)=60+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(48-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time (in hours) since noon.  \n\nAt what time is the number of people in the zoo at a maximum, and how many people are at the zoo at that time?\n\nThe maximum number of people is\n{{field-1}}\nafter\n{{field-2}}\nhours." :question/worked-solution "To find when the number of people at the zoo is at maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(60+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(48-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(60)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({\\int }_{0}^{t}(48-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(48-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =48-3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=0$:\n\n$$\n48-3{t}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,t=4\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=4.$ To find out how many people are in the zoo when $t=4,$ we figure out $N(4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(4) & =60+{\\int }_{0}^{4}(48-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =60+{[48x-{x}^{3}]|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =60+[(48\\cdot 4-{4}^{3})-(0)] \\\\  & =60+[192-64] \\\\  & =60+128 \\\\  & =188\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $60<188$, we conclude that the maximum number of people at the zoo is $188$, and this will happen after $4$ hours." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-349996/field-1" "q-349996/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3798ec35-19ef-559f-b8f8-cb0a0a03157b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-349996"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-90758/selection/27cee41df352dc5b616ccbdd499f4196361834623006386a6d8b243461c82864" :answer/id #uuid "c94e5321-5c5f-597d-b63a-81cd15d1446b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The minimum is $500$ people after $1$ hour" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-90758/selection/26f8774f77494189578a5a6d94fa76a45b7a17d811f79dc465b371ce47e3cdce" :answer/id #uuid "a684a165-a5c0-57ef-b646-8a277848e61b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The minimum is $150$ people after $5$ hours" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-90758/selection/892d23d4ade6526d557a13c333c2c371e3a280cb35bb3b2e81bbd6940627cc75" :answer/id #uuid "9dec4493-104c-5edb-a6ab-1576ffc39527" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The minimum is $100$ people after $3$ hours" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-90758/selection/03674ee798c5164b2f484209e3ceed890fa5d1989febb7189388236b3d68359f" :answer/id #uuid "76313753-5b0b-5afa-9883-ea2939d5b8af" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The minimum is $200$ people after $2$ hours" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-90758/selection/f1612026f4e6f5c6432854ba46265b25994701cf7cad4a770dd5bbb744a19db8" :answer/id #uuid "666bf0b7-2601-5a45-8e2b-8e872d3fc965" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The minimum is $200$ people after $4$ hours" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-90758/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "6af206e8-9d4e-5b40-a179-d79100d3eb32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-90758/selection/892d23d4ade6526d557a13c333c2c371e3a280cb35bb3b2e81bbd6940627cc75" :answer-field/choices ["q-90758/selection/27cee41df352dc5b616ccbdd499f4196361834623006386a6d8b243461c82864" "q-90758/selection/26f8774f77494189578a5a6d94fa76a45b7a17d811f79dc465b371ce47e3cdce" "q-90758/selection/892d23d4ade6526d557a13c333c2c371e3a280cb35bb3b2e81bbd6940627cc75" "q-90758/selection/03674ee798c5164b2f484209e3ceed890fa5d1989febb7189388236b3d68359f" "q-90758/selection/f1612026f4e6f5c6432854ba46265b25994701cf7cad4a770dd5bbb744a19db8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-90758" :question/id #uuid "c2c8878a-6a18-5df8-8fae-915f579f14bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-90758" :question/problem "The total number of people $V(t)$ who have visited a certain shopping center after $t$ hours is given by the function\n\n$$\nV(t)=1\\,000+100{\\int }_{0}^{t}(2x-6)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time (in hours) since the shopping center opened its doors to the public.\n\nAt what time is the number of people visiting the shopping center at a minimum, and how many people are in the shopping center at that time?" :question/worked-solution "To find when the number of people is at minimum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1\\,000+100{\\int }_{0}^{t}(2x-6)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1\\,000)+100\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({\\int }_{0}^{t}(2x-6)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =100\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(2x-6)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =100(2t-6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=0$:\n\n$$\n100(2t-6)=0\\,⟹\\,t=3\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=3.$ To find out how many people are in the shopping center when $t=3,$ we figure out $V(3)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(3) & =1\\,000+100{\\int }_{0}^{3}(2x-6)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1\\,000+100{[{x}^{2}-6x]|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =1\\,000+100[({3}^{2}-6(3))-(0)] \\\\  & =1\\,000-900 \\\\  & =100\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $100<1\\,000$, we conclude that the minimum number of people is $100$, and this will happen after $3$ hours." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-90758/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3798ec35-19ef-559f-b8f8-cb0a0a03157b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-90758"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3892"] :question/problem "At a shopping mall, the rate at which people are entering the mall is given by\n\n$$\nr(t) = 300t^2+50,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in people per hour, and $t>0$ is the number of hours since the mall opened. In addition, people are leaving the mall at a steady rate of $25$ people per hour. \n\n- Calculate the number of people that entered the mall in the first two hours.\n\n- Calculate the net change in the number of people inside the mall in the first 2 hours." :question/worked-solution "**Part 1**\n\nPeople are entering the mall at a rate of $r(t) = 300t^2+50.$ To find out how many people entered the mall between $t=0$ and $t=2,$ we integrate $r(t)$ between these two limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}r(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}300{t}^{2}+50\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={100{t}^{3}+50t|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(100\\cdot {2}^{3}+50(2))-(0) \\\\  & =(800+100) \\\\  & =900\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Part 2**\n\nPeople are entering the mall at a rate of $r(t) = 300t^2+50$ people per hour, and are leaving the mall at a rate of $25$ people per hour. Therefore, the **rate of change** in the number of people inside the mall, which we'll call $R(t),$ is given by:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =r(t)-25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the **net change** in the number of people, we integrate $R(t)$ between $t=0$ and $t=2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}R(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}r(t)-25\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{{\\int }_{0}^{2}r(t)\\,\\text{d}t}_{⏟}}_{900}-{\\int }_{0}^{2}25\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =900-[25t]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =900-50 \\\\  & =850\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, over the first two hours, there was a net increase of $850$ people." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3893"] :question/problem "The rate at which guests are arriving at a restaurant one evening is given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t) = 6t^2+2,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in guests per hour and $t > 0$ is the time in hours since the restaurant opened. Guests are also leaving at a steady rate of $6$ guests per hour. If there were $50$ guests in the restaurant when it opened, how many guests will be in the restaurant after $1$ hour?" :question/worked-solution "Guests are entering the restaurant at a rate of $6t^2+2$ guests per hour and are leaving at a rate of $6$ guests per hour. Therefore, the **rate of change** in the number of guests (call it $R(t)$) is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =r(t)-6 \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}+2-6 \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the **total number of guests** after $t$ hours (call it $T(t)$), we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =T(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}R(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =50+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(6{x}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that $T$ is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration. \n\nTo find out the number of guests after $1$ hour, we substitute $t=1$ into the above and evaluate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1) & =50+{\\int }_{0}^{1}(6{x}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =50+{[2{x}^{3}-4x]|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =50+[(2(1)^{3}-4(1))-(0)] \\\\  & =50-2 \\\\  & =48\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3894"] :question/problem "In example 2, we saw that the number of guests in a restaurant is given by\n\n$$\nT(t) = 50 + \\int_0^t \\left(6x^2-4\\right)\\,\\textrm d x\\,.\n$$\n\nGiven that $T(t)$ has a local minimum for some $t>0,$ find the time when the number of guests is at its minimum value." :question/worked-solution "To find the time when the number of guests is minimized, we need to solve $\\dfrac{\\textrm d T}{\\textrm d t} = 0.$\n\nDifferentiating $T(t)$ with respect to $t$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(50+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(6{x}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(50)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(6{x}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(6{x}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving $\\dfrac{\\textrm d T}{\\textrm d t} = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n6t^2-4 = 0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad t = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{2}{3}}\n$$\n\nSo we have $2$ critical points $t=\\sqrt{\\dfrac 2 3}$ and $t=0$ (the endpoint). But since the local minimum exists for $t>0,$ $t=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2}{3}}$ must be the minimum value." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
